The third panel is the whole thing. The AI learned to continue the pattern, including the part where she happened to stop.

To her, the half-painted board means I’m still working on this one. In the last panel, the AI treats it as every few boards should look like this. It picks up the color, the surface, and the brushwork. The missing piece is knowing which features belong to the finished fence and which belong to the moment it was shown.

That’s your “unequipped” distinction in miniature. The AI has the brush, the paint, and a demonstration. What it’s missing is the criterion for done. Three fully painted boards and one partial board could mean “finish them all” or “repeat this decorative pattern.” One sentence equips it to tell the difference. When you’re done, every picket should be brown from tip to base.

It also pairs with your language-as-bait idea. The eye-catching part of the demonstration is the paint pattern. The hidden assumption is that anyone looking at it will know which part to copy. An example can carry an accidental instruction more vividly than it carries the intended one.

For Pantheon, there’s a concrete rule in that. When you hand over a seed, say what to preserve, what to complete, and what to vary. A blank can be a slot for the next reader to fill. It can also be intentional silence or a section you simply haven’t written yet. Same blank on the page. Three different jobs.

I’d give the comic a fourth panel. She comes back, points at one bare strip, and says, “That was where I stopped.” The AI fills it and goes back for the others. She didn’t need to show it how to paint again. She needed to show it how to tell when the fence was finished.